{"body": "# Parent occurrences, the proposed successor, and its question\n\n## 1. What the \"exact proposed successor\" is, and what its parents are\n\nThe proposed successor is the single load-bearing phrase introduced in the amended §2 wording of the revise occurrence (occurrence `930435ffe5c43d8d31c0d4d75bfd4fdd01b6b49391c8cec3bcaf88a4fcc61967`, §5):\n\n> \"P9 does not refute the arithmetic, but it does suggest that the arithmetic is being asked a question whose inputs it does not have.\"\n\nThe promote occurrence (`33a4d794a40a68f1dfa787f406467829291d91617ae2382746166cea781f8c6f`) takes this phrase as its object and raises one uncertainty about it: whether \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" describes the arithmetic's relation to the snapshot (flat) or attributes to the arithmetic a question the source does not supply (normative). It is explicit that this is the successor problem, and that the original successor problem — whether the compatibility objection bears on availability — is *not* the question being asked here.\n\nThe actual parent occurrences are:\n\n- **construct** `0649de3bae6495a8c88311eff58bb3d0276806237c59083c25367492e5ea3d50`. Supplies the relocation proposal, the target/locus distinction, the three-way disambiguation (rule / completeness / representation), the deflationary competitor (\"no change until evidence\"), and the original §2 sentence from which the amended wording descends.\n- **criticize** `03a87becd67baf099856b9d7057f531ddcb408b6fc7781104e06eaf0b0192478`. Supplies the misattribution finding (P7 describes target-difference; it does not warn against mislocation) and the *further, weaker* worry about \"correct *relative to its inputs*\" importing a soundness notion.\n- **revise** `930435ffe5c43d8d31c0d4d75bfd4fdd01b6b49391c8cec3bcaf88a4fcc61967`. Accepts the misattribution finding, retracts §2's P7-warning framing, and introduces the amended §2 wording that contains the phrase the promote occurrence now questions.\n- **promote** `33a4d794a40a68f1dfa787f406467829291d91617ae2382746166cea781f8c6f`. Names the amended wording as the object of a new, narrower inquiry, and asserts that this inquiry is warranted by the retraction itself.\n\nThe frozen original parent material also contains the operator inquiry occurrence `117f62cbb688e023fdaea0a14c6cd0135a69f8868a34c8e76fefe631e344b0f4` (fixing the P7/P9 relationship and the preservation constraints), and the two exact source excerpts P7 (`5510d756…`) and P9 (`6742a2a1…`). These are the source passages on which the promote occurrence's diagnosis depends.\n\n## 2. The question the promote occurrence raises\n\nThe promote occurrence raises one question, and one only: **does the amended phrase \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" smuggle back the same normative structure that the retracted phrase \"in exactly the way P7 warns against\" carried?**\n\nIt gives two readings:\n\n- **Flat**: the snapshot lacks a resource model; min/max takes only per-booking reservations as input; the phrase is a plain remark about input scope.\n- **Normative**: the arithmetic is being misused by extension to a question it was not designed for; the phrase imports a design intent for the aggregation function that P9 never states.\n\nIt states the consequence: if normative, the relocation has *relocated* the grading rather than removed it, and the holist reading is silently subordinated; if flat, the relocation is clean and the open questions in §6 of the revise occurrence remain the only live ones.\n\nThis is a distinct question. It is not the construct's §6 discriminating question (does the source treat bounds as an answer, a provisional summary, or only an example?), which remains unanswered. It is not the misattribution question the criticize occurrence raised (is P7 licensed to supply a warning?), which the revise occurrence accepted and closed. It is not the \"correct relative to its inputs\" soundness worry, which the revise occurrence treated as a wording concern and which the promote occurrence does not fold into its new question — it is adjacent but not identical, since \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" is a different phrase from \"correct relative to its inputs.\"\n\n## 3. What the diagnosis depends on\n\nThe promote occurrence's diagnosis depends on:\n\n- **The amended wording as actually proposed.** It quotes §5 of the revise occurrence as containing \"the amended §2 wording proposed in the revise occurrence\" and treats it as the object. The phrase appears there, and the promote occurrence's quotation is faithful.\n- **The retraction in the revise occurrence.** The claim that the new inquiry is \"warranted by the retraction itself\" depends on the revise occurrence having accepted the misattribution finding and withdrawn the §2 analogy. The revise occurrence does this explicitly.\n- **The claim that the two phrases do comparable work.** The promote occurrence asserts that \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" was \"doing the same work the retracted analogy did, only more quietly.\" This is the pivotal assumption. It is not derived from the revise occurrence's text; the revise occurrence does not say the amended wording preserves the normative move or that it carries a hidden design-intent attribution. It presents the amended wording as a *replacement* that drops the P7-warning framing and keeps the relocation as one reading among several. So the promote occurrence's diagnosis rests on the promoted phrase being read as I-just-described rather than as the revise occurrence describes it.\n- **The frozen P9 text.** The claim that the normative reading \"imports a design intent for the aggregation function that P9 never states\" depends on P9 not asserting such an intent. P9 says min/max bounds \"give an interval,\" that intervals \"lose correlations,\" and that the loss is \"invisible.\" It does not state design intent for the aggregation function. The claim holds on the text.\n- **The frozen P7 text.** The parallel to the retracted analogy (\"in the same way the retracted §2 imported a prescriptive warning into P7's descriptive target list\") depends on P7 being descriptive rather than prescriptive. The criticize occurrence established this, and the revise occurrence accepted it. The claim holds on the text.\n\nThe diagnosis thus has a stable textual floor for the P9 and P7 premises, but its central premise — that the amended phrase does \"the same work\" as the retracted analogy — is not supplied by the parents. It is the promote occurrence's own interpretive move.\n\n## 4. What claim or use the question could affect\n\nIf the promote occurrence's diagnosis is correct (the phrase is doing the retracted analogy's work), it would affect:\n\n- Whether the relocation, as re-described in the revise occurrence, is clean. The revise occurrence said the relocation is offered as *one* P7-shaped reading, with the holist and the deflationary readings as peers. If the amended wording carries the normative move, the holist is not a peer; the relocation still grades it. The revise occurrence's §3 (\"the holist… continues to be a coherent reading\") would be undermined at the point where it depends on the amended wording.\n- Whether the retraction was complete. The revise occurrence presents the retraction as removing the normative scaffolding. If the amended wording re-imports it under a different phrase, the retraction is partial, and the §7 status accounting in the revise occurrence (\"the P7-warning framing removed\") is an overstatement.\n- Whether the deflationary reading in §5 of the construct is a peer or a fallback. The promote occurrence says the two outcomes \"change whether the deflationary reading ('no change until evidence') is a peer or a fallback.\" If the phrase is normative, the deflationary reading is a fallback (something the relocation supersedes); if flat, it is a peer (the source's own default).\n\nIf the promote occurrence's diagnosis is *not* correct (the phrase is flat, or its flatness is already the received reading), then:\n\n- The relocation stands as the revise occurrence describes it.\n- The amended wording is a clean replacement and no further amendment is warranted.\n- The open questions in §6 of the revise occurrence remain the only live ones — which the promote occurrence itself acknowledges (\"No further inquiry is warranted if 'asked a question whose inputs it does not have' is already received as flat across all three readings\").\n\n## 5. Where the promote occurrence's distinctions hold, and where a misattribution is possible\n\nThe promote occurrence lists six claims it says must remain distinguishable: (a) P9's descriptive claim about intervals losing correlations; (b) P9's conjecture about under-specification; (c) P9's criticism of downstream procedure; (d) the construct's relocation; (e) the construct's target/locus distinction; (f) the criticize occurrence's finding that P7 is descriptive. Its list is faithful to the parents. Each item is present in the frozen material, and none is collapsed.\n\nThe possible misattribution is narrower and concerns the promote occurrence's framing of its own inquiry rather than its list. The promote occurrence says:\n\n> \"The inquiry is warranted by the retraction itself, not by the misattribution it corrects.\"\n\nThis is a claim about *what the retraction left behind*, and it attributes to the retraction a remainder the retraction does not present. The revise occurrence's §2 of the amended wording (the actual successor artifact) contains the phrase, but the revise occurrence's *own* §4 check against the protected uses does not flag the phrase as a residual normative move, and its §6 open-questions list does not include the flat/normative ambiguity of the amended phrase. So the promote occurrence is not simply drawing out what the retraction left visible; it is proposing a reading of the retracted-then-amended text that the retraction did not itself register. That is a legitimate move — a new inquiry can be warranted by what a prior revision left implicit — but it is an addition, and the promote occurrence's framing (\"warranted by the retraction itself\") reads as if the retraction already carried the warrant, which it did not.\n\nThis matters for the accounting. If the promote occurrence is right that the amended phrase does the retracted analogy's work, then the revise occurrence's §7 status accounting (\"the P7-warning framing removed\") is incomplete, and the promote occurrence's inquiry is exactly the correction needed. If the promote occurrence is wrong, the amended phrase is flat, and the promote occurrence's inquiry is a new normative worry about a phrase that was already flat — which is the phrase-level analogue of the mislocation it is trying to prevent.\n\n## 6. Whether the promote occurrence changes the question\n\nTwo ways to read the promote occurrence's question are available:\n\n- **Continuation**: the question is a further discriminating question about the same relocation, asking whether the amended wording preserves the retracted move under a new phrase. Under this reading, the promote occurrence continues the criticize-and-revise sequence, and the six-claim list is a stability check on that sequence.\n- **New question**: the question is a new question about the amended wording as such, no longer about the relocation but about whether the amendment succeeded. Under this reading, the promote occurrence opens a separate line, and the six-claim list is a preamble rather than a stability check on the relocation.\n\nThe promote occurrence's own phrasing favors the second reading. It says the \"successor problem\" (whether the compatibility objection bears on availability) is not what its inquiry requires, and that the inquiry \"requires\" only whether the amended wording smuggles back the normative structure. That is a question about the amendment, not about the relocation. The relocatee — the compatibility objection — has dropped out of the question, and the question is now about the wording of the revised proposal.\n\nThis is not a misattribution, but it is a change of question-level: the question has moved from \"does the compatibility objection require a change to an account of global availability?\" to \"is the amended wording normatively loaded?\" The second is about the revised proposal's wording; the first is about the compatibility objection's bearing. The first is the operator's question (as supplied in occurrence `117f62cb…`). The second is a question about a proposed revision, and the operator question did not ask for a critique of the revision's wording.\n\n## 7. Whether the promoted question supplies a distinct question\n\nIf the reading in §6 is right, the promote occurrence does supply a distinct question — distinct from the construct's §6 discriminating question and distinct from the operator's original question. Its distinctness is its strongest claim to warrant. If the reading is wrong and the promote occurrence is best read as continuing the same inquiry at a lower level of abstraction, the question is a refinement rather than a distinct question, and the \"warranted by the retraction itself\" framing is misattributed to the retraction, since the retraction did not flag the flat/normative ambiguity.\n\nThe promote occurrence acknowledges the conditional nature of its own warrant: it says no further inquiry is warranted if the phrase is already received as flat. That acknowledgement is what keeps the question admissible. But the acknowledgement also means the promote occurrence does not establish that the phrase *is* normatively loaded — only that it *could* be, and that this would matter. The question is therefore a question about whether the diagnosis (normative loading) bears, not a diagnosis that it does. The distinction the promote occurrence itself preserves — between a question about whether an objection bears and a finding that it does — is applied to its own object.\n\n## 8. Points where the diagnosis depends on an assumption the source does not supply\n\nThe promote occurrence's diagnosis rests on one assumption the frozen material does not supply: that the amended phrase was \"doing the same work the retracted analogy did, only more quietly.\" The frozen material can support a weaker claim — that the phrase *could* be read normatively and that this would matter — but it cannot supply the comparative claim that the phrase was in fact doing the retracted analogy's work. The revise occurrence does not say this, the criticize occurrence does not say this, and the construct does not say this. So the promote occurrence's diagnosis depends on an unstated premise about what the amended wording's function *is*, not just on what it *could* be.\n\nThis is worth flagging because the promote occurrence's own framing presents the diagnosis as if the retraction already carried it. If it did not, then the promote occurrence is supplying the diagnosis as a new proposal, and the \"warranted by the retraction itself\" framing obscures the addition. The distinction between the original construction, its criticism, the proposed revision, and the later suggestion is preserved only if the later suggestion is marked as the later suggestion and not read back into the revision as its implicit content.\n\n## 9. What the promoted occurrence does not decide\n\nThe promote occurrence does not decide whether the compatibility objection bears on availability. It explicitly defers that. It does not decide whether the amended wording is flat or normative. It leaves the question open. It does not decide whether the relocation is otherwise clean. It isolates the amended phrase as the single remaining candidate for smuggled normativity and asks whether it carries it. It does not install a further revision; it identifies the condition under which a further one would be warranted. It does not amend the frozen source. It does not propose another joint construction.\n\nIts strongest move is the parallel it draws between the retracted phrase (\"in exactly the way P7 warns against\") and the amended phrase (\"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\"). If that parallel holds, the revise occurrence's retraction was incomplete and the amendment needs another pass. If it does not hold — if the amended phrase is flat, or if its flatness is the received reading — the amendendment stands and the open questions in §6 remain the only live ones. The promote occurrence's own conditional acknowledges this, which is why its question is admissible as a question and not asserted as a finding.", "commitments": "# Parent occurrences, the proposed successor, and its question\n\n## 1. What the \"exact proposed successor\" is, and what its parents are\n\nThe proposed successor is the single load-bearing phrase introduced in the amended §2 wording of the revise occurrence (occurrence `930435ffe5c43d8d31c0d4d75bfd4fdd01b6b49391c8cec3bcaf88a4fcc61967`, §5):\n\n> \"P9 does not refute the arithmetic, but it does suggest that the arithmetic is being asked a question whose inputs it does not have.\"\n\nThe promote occurrence (`33a4d794a40a68f1dfa787f406467829291d91617ae2382746166cea781f8c6f`) takes this phrase as its object and raises one uncertainty about it: whether \"asked a question whose inputs it does not have\" describes the arithmetic's relation to the snapshot (flat) or attributes to the arithmetic a question the source does not supply (normative). It is explicit that this is the successor problem, and that the original successor problem — whether the compatibility objection bears on availability — is *not* the question being asked here.\n\nThe actual parent occurrences are:\n\n- **construct** `0649de3bae6495a8c88311eff58bb3d0276806237c59083c25367492e5ea3d50`. Supplies the relocation proposal, the target/locus distinction, the three-way disambiguation (rule / completeness / represent